summary_en.experiments:
The evaluation spans two Transformer families, Llama and Qwen, and two linear block types, Mamba-2 and GDN, which helps show that the recipe is not tied to a single base model. The clearest quantitative claims in the provided text are:
1. staged long-context training is extended from the roughly 24K regime common in prior upcycling work to 64K;
2. after integration into vLLM with tensor parallelism, a Llama-3.2-3B-scale HyLo model can serve up to 2M tokens on 8 AMD MI300X GPUs, about a 30x context extension over the original model.

Empirically, the paper claims better long-context performance than Zebra-Llama-style baselines while keeping short-context quality comparable. A particularly useful part of the study is the analysis of how training sequence length affects long-context generalization. That is more informative than simply reporting a final training length, because it probes whether longer training contexts actually extrapolate and how the curriculum schedule affects stability.

Another key result is the benefit of teacher-guided distillation. Chunk-wise KL supervision significantly improves long-context performance and stabilizes optimization after upcycling. Even without all benchmark tables here, the main pattern is clear:
- architecture replacement alone is not enough to reliably obtain strong long-context behavior;
- staged context-length training helps;
- adding teacher-guided distillation improves long-context quality further;
- these trends broadly hold across different base models and linear modules.

The most useful takeaway is not a single score but the decomposition of long-context gains into three factors: architecture, length curriculum, and distillation.

summary_en.reading_guide:
Although tagged as skim, this is close to a must-read if you work on architecture or continued pretraining. A good reading order is:
1. read the introduction and the early figures to understand the dual objective of preserving short-context quality while improving long-context ability;
2. read the architecture section carefully, especially how MLA is combined with Mamba-2 or GDN and why the MLA ratio matters;
3. read the initialization details, with special attention to Transformer-to-GDN transfer, since stable upcycling depends heavily on this step;
4. then read the staged long-context training and chunk-wise KL distillation sections;
5. in the experiments, prioritize ablations over training length, distillation settings, base model family, and linear block choice.

Roughly 45 to 60 minutes should be enough for the main ideas. The most interesting open questions are whether long-context gains come mainly from retained attention anchors or from the linear state model, whether teacher distillation limits the hybrid model from learning a better inductive bias than the Transformer teacher, and how well this recipe scales to stronger and larger checkpoints.

summary_en.experiments:
On bug localization and code generation benchmarks, CodeComp consistently outperforms attention-only KV compression baselines (e.g., SnapKV, ParallelComp) under equal KV memory budgets. Motivation experiments show:
- Strong misalignment between attention importance and CPG-based structural importance (top-20% chunk Jaccard ≈ 0.0944);
- Attention-only compression prunes a substantial fraction of structurally critical tokens across categories;
- Structure-aware selection retains more critical tokens across all categories and recovers most full-context accuracy under aggressive compression; patch generation quality can match uncompressed full-context inference (as stated in the abstract).
